### Costing rules (ceilings vs fixed values — do not confuse the two)
- Ticket cost: **ceiling** 0.38 (warn if over, never send as a value).
- Assembly charge: **ceiling** 0.75 (warn if over).
- Metal loss: **ceiling** 5% (warn if over).
- Casting cost: **variable by item, intentionally no default** — left null everywhere.
- Finishing: **fixed value** — all items get "high polish", cost 1. Set on all 8 `category` rows.
- Dropship: **fixed value** 1.50 when dropshipped — belongs on vendor cost's `dropshipFee`. **Not wired in anywhere yet** — vendor cost has no write implementation (see Pending below).

These ceilings are implemented as flat global constants (same for every item, not per-type) in the labor block of `sspCreate.js`.

## Key code — `src/utils/sspClient.js`
Thin wrappers per SSP endpoint: `sspSaveHeader`, `sspSetCostingMethod`, `sspSetTethers`, `sspCreateItem`, `sspUpdateItem`, `sspAddMaterial`, `sspUpdateMaterial`, `sspGetItemMaterials` (GET; treats 204 as empty array — the phantom-success guard), `sspAddStone`, `sspStageImage(s)`, `sspGetHeader`, `sspGetItemFilters`, `sspGetItemFindings`, `sspAddFinding`, `sspUpdateFinding`, `sspUpdateLaborCost`, `sspGetLaborCost`.

## Key code — `src/utils/sspCreate.js`
The main payload-building/orchestration file.

- `categoryFor(sample)` — three-tier resolution: (1) legacy direct fields if set, (2) the view's resolved `type_ssp_product_type`/`type_default_category`, (3) legacy `CATEGORY_TO_SSP` map keyed on resolved type NAME. Emits warnings by source.
- `PLM_COLOR_TO_SSP` + `platingsForSample(sample)` — plating color comes from the **item's own color field**, not a per-layer color ("the thing is based off the color of the item. plating is just plating" — Chaim). Falls back to the layer's stored color only when the item has no signal (e.g. black rhodium).
- Selling-type: stored values are SSP's own words (`pairs|piece|set`, migrated from the old `pair|single|set`) → `effectiveSellsAs` → `quantityType` (direct passthrough) → `piecesPerUnit` (`piece` → 1, else 2).
- Weight: `const weight = n(sample.salesWeight) ?? n(sample.weight);` — sales weight preferred, per explicit instruction.
- **Finding block** — builds `finding` object when `sample.finding_type` is set; metal cost fields (`metalCostPerGram`, `findingMetalLossAmt`, fixing allowance) derived from `sample.finding_net_weight` using the formula above; `quantity: effectiveSellsAs === "pairs" ? 2 : 1`; warns if no finding is set on an earrings/charms type ("SSP reports 'Missing Component' without one").
- **Labor block** — reads `sample.casting_cost`, `sample.assembly_charge`, `sample.labor_per_gram`, `sample.finishing_type`/`finishing_cost`; ceiling warnings for assembly>0.75, ticket>0.38, metal_loss>5%.
- `costingMethod: s(sample.costing_method) || d.costingMethod` — per-metal, not global.
- `unitOfMeasure: s(sample.unit_of_measure) || "mandrel size"` — per-type.
- `productComponent` on the item payload declares `"Finding"` when a finding exists, alongside `"Material"`/`"Stone"`.
- `sendPreparedSspCreates` — orchestrates the full send: header → item → material (GET-first, create/update, verify-after) → finding (same GET-first/verify pattern) → labor cost (`sspUpdateLaborCost` then `sspGetLaborCost` to verify `ttlAllLaborCosts` came back non-null).

